{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Decision material for the \"notice a stale override\" AC — measured 2026-08-05\n\nThe AC asks for \"a mechanism ... to notice an override whose REMOVE-WHEN condition has been met\n... either a periodic check or a documented review trigger\". Three facts make that choice\ncheaper than it looks.\n\n## 1. The override mechanism already exists and is documented\n\n`.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml:178` documents it in the pip-audit job's own comment:\na finding may be \"silenced with `--ignore-vuln <GHSA/PYSEC-ID>` below and a one-line\n[justification]\". The failure message at :233 surfaces it to authors directly:\n\n    ::error::pip-audit found dependency vulnerabilities (see output). Bump the pinned version,\n    or add --ignore-vuln <ID> with justification if accepted.\n\nSo no new override convention needs inventing. Whatever is chosen just has to notice staleness\nin THIS mechanism.\n\n## 2. There are currently ZERO active overrides — this AC is PREVENTIVE, not remedial\n\nSwept the workflows: the only occurrences of `--ignore-vuln` are the explanatory comment at :178\nand the error text at :233. No invocation actually passes the flag today.\n\nThat matters for scoping. There is no backlog of stale suppressions to sweep, and no urgency\ndriven by existing debt. The work is to ensure the FIRST override does not become permanent by\ndefault — which is a much smaller job than auditing accumulated ones, and argues for doing it\nnow while the cost is near zero.\n\n## 3. A periodic host already exists, with a direct precedent\n\nSeven scheduled workflows are already in place:\n\n    codeql.yml                  23 4 * * 1     weekly\n    external-integration.yml    0 6 * * 1      weekly\n    mirror-guard.yml            */15 * * * *   every 15 min\n    prompt-eval.yml             0 7 * * 1      weekly drift detector\n    reconcile-bridge-canary.yml 0 * * * *      hourly\n    reconcile-bridge.yml        */20 * * * *\n    terraform-drift.yml         17 6 * * *     daily\n\n`terraform-drift.yml`'s own comment reads \"daily drift sweep even without a code change\" — which\nis EXACTLY the shape this AC describes, already accepted and running in this repo. So \"periodic\ncheck\" is not a new mechanism to justify; it is an established pattern with a sibling.\n\n`prompt-eval.yml` is a second precedent, self-described as a \"weekly drift detector\".\n\n## Recommendation, given the above\n\n**Periodic check, not a documented review trigger.** A documented trigger relies on someone\nremembering to look, which is the same failure mode as the hard cap in story 5e53 — where every\ninstance was discovered by an already-blocked contributor rather than by anyone checking. The\nperiodic pattern is already precedented here twice, and with zero current overrides the\nimplementation is a re-audit that passes trivially until the first suppression exists.\n\n**Possible consolidation worth considering:** task 03ef-6fb5-158b-4abd proposes a scheduled\nmain-health run for a different reason (mirror CI cancellations). If that is adopted, it is a\nnatural host for this check rather than adding an eighth schedule. Worth deciding the two\ntogether rather than separately.\n\n## What I did NOT verify — do not treat as settled\n\nThe pip-audit job's comment says it scans \"the base runtime install (no extras) — that is what a\nplain `pip install nava-rebar` consumer gets\". But the incident this ticket describes came via\n`inspect-ai`, which lives in an extra, capping `click`. I did not reconcile how an extra-only\ncapping dependency produces a finding in a base-only scan; the ticket's own explanation is that\n\"uv's universal resolution locks every extra jointly\". Someone should confirm that before\ndesigning the check, because it determines WHAT the periodic job must actually audit — the base\ninstall, the full lock, or both."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785919766205017001,"uuid":"aa8df346-df20-4ce9-8db3-ca07f594e765"}